ITEMS = [
    # ---------------- Basics and the JVM ----------------
    Q('jdk-jre-jvm', 'Basics and JVM', 'e',
      'What is the difference between the JDK, the JRE and the JVM?',
      'Think of which one you need to <b>write</b> code, which one to <b>run</b> it, and which one actually executes the bytecode.',
      'The JVM runs bytecode. The JRE is the JVM plus the core class libraries needed to run programs. The JDK is the JRE plus development tools such as <code>javac</code>, <code>jar</code> and <code>javadoc</code>.',
      '<p>The compiler <code>javac</code> turns <code>.java</code> source into <code>.class</code> bytecode. Bytecode is not tied to any operating system. The JVM for each platform loads that bytecode, verifies it, and runs it with an interpreter plus a JIT compiler that turns hot code into native machine code.</p><p>Since Java 11 Oracle no longer ships a separate JRE download, so in practice you install a JDK everywhere. The idea still matters because it explains why Java is "write once, run anywhere": the bytecode is portable and the JVM is the part that is platform specific.</p>',
      points=['JVM: executes bytecode, platform specific', 'JRE: JVM plus libraries, enough to run', 'JDK: JRE plus compiler and tools, needed to develop'],
      follow=['Is the JVM platform independent?', 'What does the JIT compiler do?']),
    Q('platform-independent', 'Basics and JVM', 'e',
      'Why is Java called platform independent?',
      'What file does the compiler produce, and who reads that file?',
      'Because Java source is compiled to bytecode, not to machine code. The same <code>.class</code> file runs on any operating system that has a JVM.',
      '<p>A C program compiled on Windows produces a Windows executable. A Java program compiled on Windows produces bytecode, which runs unchanged on Linux or macOS. The JVM itself is platform dependent, so there is one JVM build per platform, and that is what makes the bytecode portable.</p>',
      follow=['Then is the JVM platform independent too?']),
    Q('main-static', 'Basics and JVM', 'e',
      'Why is the <code>main</code> method <code>public static void</code>? What happens if you remove <code>static</code>?',
      'The JVM has to call <code>main</code> before any object of your class exists.',
      '<code>public</code> so the JVM can reach it from outside the class, <code>static</code> so it can be called without creating an object, and <code>void</code> because it returns nothing to the JVM. Without <code>static</code>, older JDKs compile the class but refuse to launch it with an error saying the main method is not static. Since Java 25 the launcher accepts an instance <code>main</code> too, creating the object first, but the classic signature is still what interviewers expect.',
      '<p>The launcher looks for the exact signature <code>public static void main(String[] args)</code>. You can write <code>String... args</code> or <code>final String[] args</code> and it still works. You can also overload <code>main</code>, but only the standard signature is used as the entry point.</p><p>To end a program with a status code use <code>System.exit(code)</code>, since <code>main</code> cannot return one.</p>',
      follow=['Can we overload main?', 'Can main be declared final or synchronized?']),
    Q('primitive-wrapper', 'Basics and JVM', 'e',
      'What are the eight primitive types and why do wrapper classes exist?',
      'Collections such as <code>ArrayList</code> can only hold objects.',
      'The primitives are <code>byte</code>, <code>short</code>, <code>int</code>, <code>long</code>, <code>float</code>, <code>double</code>, <code>char</code> and <code>boolean</code>. Wrapper classes such as <code>Integer</code> and <code>Double</code> let a primitive be used where an object is required, for example in collections and generics, and they add helper methods like <code>Integer.parseInt</code>.',
      '<p>Java converts between the two automatically. Turning an <code>int</code> into an <code>Integer</code> is called autoboxing, and the reverse is unboxing. Two traps follow from this. Unboxing a <code>null</code> wrapper throws <code>NullPointerException</code>, and comparing two wrappers with <code>==</code> compares references, not values.</p>',
      points=['Sizes: byte 1, short 2, int 4, long 8, float 4, double 8, char 2 bytes', 'boolean size is JVM dependent', 'Wrappers are immutable']),
    Q('integer-cache', 'Basics and JVM', 'm', 'What does this print and why?',
      'Integer objects between -128 and 127 are reused from a cache.',
      'It prints <code>true</code> then <code>false</code>. Autoboxing uses <code>Integer.valueOf</code>, which returns cached objects for values from -128 to 127. So <code>a</code> and <code>b</code> are the same object, while <code>c</code> and <code>d</code> are two different objects and <code>==</code> compares references.',
      '<p>This is why you should always compare wrapper values with <code>equals</code> or unbox them first. The upper end of the cache can be raised with the JVM option <code>-XX:AutoBoxCacheMax</code>, so code that relies on <code>==</code> may behave differently on another machine.</p>',
      kind='Output',
      code='public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Integer a = 127, b = 127;\n        Integer c = 128, d = 128;\n        System.out.println(a == b);\n        System.out.println(c == d);\n    }\n}',
      acode='true\nfalse'),
    Q('pass-by-value', 'Basics and JVM', 'm',
      'Is Java pass by value or pass by reference? Explain with an object.',
      'What exactly is copied when you pass an object variable to a method?',
      'Java is always pass by value. For objects, the value that is copied is the reference. The method can change the object the reference points to, but reassigning the parameter does not change the caller\'s variable.',
      '<p>In the code below, <code>rename</code> changes the shared object, so the caller sees the new name. <code>replace</code> points its local copy of the reference at a new object, which the caller never sees.</p>',
      acode='class Box { String name = "old"; }\n\nstatic void rename(Box b)  { b.name = "new"; }   // caller sees "new"\nstatic void replace(Box b) { b = new Box(); b.name = "other"; } // caller still sees "new"',
      follow=['How would you swap two Integer variables through a method?']),
    Q('stack-heap', 'Basics and JVM', 'm',
      'What is stored on the stack and what is stored on the heap?',
      'Each thread has its own one of these, and all threads share the other.',
      'Each thread has its own stack, which holds method frames with local variables, primitive values and references. Objects and arrays live on the heap, which all threads share and the garbage collector manages.',
      '<p>When a method returns, its frame is popped and its locals disappear at once. Objects on the heap stay until nothing reachable refers to them. Deep or endless recursion fills the stack and throws <code>StackOverflowError</code>. Creating too many live objects fills the heap and throws <code>OutOfMemoryError</code>.</p><p>Static variables and class metadata live in the method area, which HotSpot calls Metaspace since Java 8.</p>',
      points=['Stack: per thread, frames, locals, fast, freed on return', 'Heap: shared, objects, managed by GC']),
    Q('gc', 'Basics and JVM', 'm',
      'How does garbage collection work in Java? Can you force it?',
      'Think about reachability from GC roots, and about what <code>System.gc()</code> really promises.',
      'The garbage collector frees objects that can no longer be reached from GC roots such as thread stacks, static fields and JNI references. You can request a collection with <code>System.gc()</code>, but the JVM is free to ignore it, so you cannot force it.',
      '<p>Most collectors are generational. New objects go to the young generation, where most of them die quickly and are collected cheaply. Objects that survive several collections move to the old generation. Modern JVMs use G1 by default, with ZGC and Shenandoah for very low pause times.</p><p>An object becomes eligible when its last reference is set to <code>null</code>, goes out of scope, or is reassigned. <code>finalize</code> is deprecated, so use try with resources or <code>Cleaner</code> to release resources.</p>',
      follow=['What is a memory leak in Java if GC exists?', 'What are weak and soft references?']),
    Q('classloader', 'Basics and JVM', 'h',
      'What are class loaders and what is the parent delegation model?',
      'There are three built in loaders, and each asks someone else first.',
      'Class loaders load <code>.class</code> files into the JVM. The built in ones are the bootstrap loader (core JDK classes), the platform loader and the application loader (your classpath). With parent delegation, a loader first asks its parent to load a class and only loads it itself if the parent cannot.',
      '<p>Delegation stops your code from replacing core classes, because a request for <code>java.lang.String</code> always reaches the bootstrap loader first. The same class loaded by two different loaders counts as two different types, which is the cause of puzzling <code>ClassCastException</code> errors in app servers and plugin systems.</p>'),

    # ---------------- OOP ----------------
    Q('oop-pillars', 'OOP', 'e',
      'Explain the four pillars of OOP with an example from a test automation framework.',
      'Think of a Page Object class, a BaseTest class, the WebDriver interface and private locators.',
      'Encapsulation hides data behind methods, inheritance reuses code from a parent class, polymorphism lets one reference take many forms, and abstraction exposes what an object does while hiding how.',
      '<table><tr><th>Pillar</th><th>Where it appears in a Selenium framework</th></tr><tr><td>Encapsulation</td><td>Locators in a Page Object are <code>private</code>, and tests call public methods such as <code>login(user, pass)</code>.</td></tr><tr><td>Inheritance</td><td>Every test class extends <code>BaseTest</code>, which opens and closes the browser.</td></tr><tr><td>Polymorphism</td><td><code>WebDriver driver = new ChromeDriver()</code> can become <code>new FirefoxDriver()</code> without changing the tests. Method overloading in utilities is compile time polymorphism.</td></tr><tr><td>Abstraction</td><td><code>WebDriver</code> is an interface. Tests use its methods without knowing how each browser driver works.</td></tr></table>',
      kind='Scenario', follow=['Where have you used method overriding in your framework?']),
    Q('overload-override', 'OOP', 'e',
      'What is the difference between method overloading and method overriding?',
      'One is decided by the compiler, the other while the program runs.',
      'Overloading means the same method name with different parameter lists in the same class, chosen at compile time. Overriding means a subclass gives its own body for a method with the same signature as the parent, chosen at run time based on the actual object.',
      '<p>Rules for overriding: same name and parameters, a return type that is the same or a subtype (covariant return), access that is the same or wider, and no new or broader checked exceptions. <code>static</code>, <code>private</code> and <code>final</code> methods cannot be overridden. A static method with the same signature in a subclass hides the parent method instead.</p><p>Return type alone cannot overload a method, since the call <code>m()</code> would be ambiguous.</p>',
      points=['Overloading: compile time, same class, different parameters', 'Overriding: run time, subclass, same signature', 'Use @Override so the compiler checks it']),
    Q('abstract-interface', 'OOP', 'm',
      'When would you choose an abstract class over an interface?',
      'Consider state, constructors and how many types a class can extend.',
      'Use an abstract class when related classes share state and some implementation, and you want a constructor. Use an interface to define a capability that unrelated classes can implement, since a class can implement many interfaces but extend only one class.',
      '<p>Since Java 8 interfaces can have <code>default</code> and <code>static</code> methods, and since Java 9 <code>private</code> methods, so the line is about state. Interface fields are always <code>public static final</code> constants. An abstract class can have instance fields, any access modifiers and constructors that subclasses call with <code>super</code>.</p><p>In a framework, <code>BasePage</code> is often abstract because it holds the driver and wait objects. A <code>Reportable</code> or <code>Retryable</code> capability fits an interface.</p>',
      follow=['Can an abstract class have no abstract methods?', 'What happens if two interfaces have the same default method?']),
    Q('diamond-default', 'OOP', 'h',
      'A class implements two interfaces that both define <code>default void log()</code>. What happens and how do you fix it?',
      'The compiler refuses to pick one for you.',
      'The class does not compile until it overrides <code>log()</code>. Inside the override it can call one specific version with <code>A.super.log()</code> or <code>B.super.log()</code>.',
      '<p>This is how Java handles the diamond problem for behaviour. Java avoids multiple inheritance of state entirely, since interfaces cannot have instance fields. If one of the methods comes from a superclass instead of an interface, the class method wins, following the rule "class wins over interface".</p>',
      acode='class Logger implements A, B {\n    @Override\n    public void log() {\n        A.super.log();\n    }\n}'),
    Q('constructor-chain', 'OOP', 'm',
      'What is constructor chaining? What is the difference between <code>this()</code> and <code>super()</code>?',
      'Both must be the first statement in a constructor.',
      'Constructor chaining is one constructor calling another. <code>this(...)</code> calls another constructor of the same class, and <code>super(...)</code> calls a constructor of the parent class. Either one must be the first statement, so you cannot use both in the same constructor.',
      '<p>If you write neither, the compiler inserts <code>super()</code>. If the parent has no no argument constructor, that inserted call fails to compile, which is a common interview trap. Constructors are not inherited and cannot be overridden, but they can be overloaded.</p>'),
    Q('static-keyword', 'OOP', 'e',
      'Explain the <code>static</code> keyword for variables, methods, blocks and nested classes.',
      'Static things belong to the class, not to each object.',
      'A static variable has one copy shared by all objects. A static method can be called on the class without an object and cannot use <code>this</code> or instance fields directly. A static block runs once when the class is loaded. A static nested class does not need an outer object.',
      '<p>In frameworks, static is used for utility methods (<code>WaitUtils.waitForVisible</code>), constants, and sometimes a shared driver. A static <code>WebDriver</code> breaks parallel runs, because every thread overwrites the same variable. Use <code>ThreadLocal&lt;WebDriver&gt;</code> instead.</p>',
      follow=['Why can a static method not be overridden?']),
    Q('final-finally-finalize', 'OOP', 'e',
      'What is the difference between <code>final</code>, <code>finally</code> and <code>finalize</code>?',
      'One is a keyword for declarations, one is a block, one is an old method.',
      '<code>final</code> makes a variable unchangeable once assigned, a method impossible to override and a class impossible to extend. <code>finally</code> is a block after <code>try</code> that runs whether or not an exception is thrown. <code>finalize</code> was an <code>Object</code> method called before garbage collection and is deprecated since Java 9.',
      '<p>A <code>final</code> reference cannot point to another object, but the object itself can still change. A <code>final List</code> can still have items added. To make it unchangeable use <code>List.of(...)</code> or <code>Collections.unmodifiableList</code>.</p>'),
    Q('equals-hashcode', 'OOP', 'm',
      'What is the contract between <code>equals</code> and <code>hashCode</code>? What breaks if you override only <code>equals</code>?',
      'Think about how a HashMap finds the bucket before it calls equals.',
      'If two objects are equal by <code>equals</code>, they must return the same <code>hashCode</code>. If you override only <code>equals</code>, two equal objects can land in different buckets, so a <code>HashSet</code> keeps duplicates and <code>map.get</code> cannot find a key that is equal to the one you stored.',
      '<p>The reverse is not required. Two unequal objects may share a hash code, which is just a collision. A good <code>hashCode</code> uses the same fields as <code>equals</code>, for example <code>Objects.hash(id, email)</code>. Records generate both methods for you from their components.</p>',
      acode='@Override public boolean equals(Object o) {\n    if (this == o) return true;\n    if (!(o instanceof User u)) return false;\n    return id == u.id && Objects.equals(email, u.email);\n}\n@Override public int hashCode() { return Objects.hash(id, email); }'),
    Q('immutable-class', 'OOP', 'h',
      'How do you create an immutable class in Java?',
      'Think final, private, no setters, and defensive copies.',
      'Declare the class <code>final</code>, make all fields <code>private final</code>, set them only in the constructor, provide no setters, and make defensive copies of mutable fields both when they come in and when they go out.',
      '<p>Immutable objects are thread safe without locks, are safe as map keys and can be cached. <code>String</code>, the wrapper classes and <code>LocalDate</code> are immutable. Since Java 16, a <code>record</code> gives you most of this for free, but you still need to copy mutable components such as lists.</p>',
      acode='public final class TestUser {\n    private final String name;\n    private final List<String> roles;\n\n    public TestUser(String name, List<String> roles) {\n        this.name = name;\n        this.roles = List.copyOf(roles);   // defensive copy\n    }\n    public String name() { return name; }\n    public List<String> roles() { return roles; } // already unmodifiable\n}'),
    Q('composition', 'OOP', 'm',
      'What is the difference between inheritance and composition? Which do you prefer?',
      'IS A versus HAS A.',
      'Inheritance is an IS A relationship where a subclass extends a parent. Composition is a HAS A relationship where a class holds a reference to another object and delegates to it. Prefer composition in most cases because it keeps classes loosely coupled and lets you swap parts at run time.',
      '<p>Deep inheritance trees in test frameworks make every page depend on everything above it. With composition a <code>LoginPage</code> can hold a <code>HeaderComponent</code> and a <code>WaitHelper</code> instead of inheriting from a giant base class.</p>'),
    Q('access-modifiers', 'OOP', 'e',
      'List the access modifiers from most to least restrictive and say where each is visible.',
      'There are four, and one of them has no keyword.',
      '<code>private</code> is visible only in the class. Default (no keyword, package private) is visible in the same package. <code>protected</code> is visible in the same package and in subclasses in other packages. <code>public</code> is visible everywhere.',
      '<p>A top level class can only be <code>public</code> or default. When overriding, you can keep or widen access but never narrow it, so a <code>protected</code> method can become <code>public</code> in the subclass, but not <code>private</code>.</p>'),
    Q('polymorphism-output', 'OOP', 'm', 'What does this print?',
      'Fields are chosen by the reference type, methods by the object type.',
      'It prints <code>Animal</code> and then <code>Dog barks</code>. Fields are not polymorphic, so <code>a.name</code> uses the declared type <code>Animal</code>. The overridden method is chosen at run time from the actual object, which is a <code>Dog</code>.',
      '<p>This is called field hiding. The subclass field <code>name</code> does not replace the parent field, both exist inside the object. Use getter methods if you want polymorphic behaviour.</p>',
      kind='Output',
      code='public class Main {\n    static class Animal { String name = "Animal"; String sound() { return "Animal sound"; } }\n    static class Dog extends Animal { String name = "Dog"; String sound() { return "Dog barks"; } }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Animal a = new Dog();\n        System.out.println(a.name);\n        System.out.println(a.sound());\n    }\n}',
      acode='Animal\nDog barks'),

    # ---------------- Strings ----------------
    Q('string-immutable', 'Strings', 'e',
      'Why is <code>String</code> immutable in Java?',
      'Think about the string pool, security, hash codes and threads.',
      'Strings are immutable so they can be shared safely in the string pool, used as reliable <code>HashMap</code> keys with a cached hash code, passed between threads without locks, and trusted for security sensitive values such as file paths, URLs and class names.',
      '<p>Every method that seems to change a string, such as <code>toUpperCase</code> or <code>replace</code>, returns a new string. A classic trap is calling <code>s.concat("x")</code> without assigning the result, which leaves <code>s</code> unchanged.</p>'),
    Q('string-pool', 'Strings', 'e',
      'What is the difference between <code>String s = "hi"</code> and <code>new String("hi")</code>? How many objects are created?',
      'One of them always creates a fresh object on the heap.',
      'A literal is stored in the string pool and reused, so two literals with the same text are the same object. <code>new String("hi")</code> always creates a new heap object, plus the literal in the pool if it is not already there. So it creates one or two objects.',
      '<p><code>intern()</code> returns the pooled copy of a string. Since Java 7 the pool lives on the main heap, so it is garbage collected like other objects.</p>',
      code='String a = "hi";\nString b = "hi";\nString c = new String("hi");\nSystem.out.println(a == b);       // true\nSystem.out.println(a == c);       // false\nSystem.out.println(a.equals(c));  // true'),
    Q('sb-sbuffer', 'Strings', 'e',
      'Compare <code>String</code>, <code>StringBuilder</code> and <code>StringBuffer</code>.',
      'Mutability and thread safety.',
      '<code>String</code> is immutable. <code>StringBuilder</code> is mutable and not synchronized, so it is the fastest choice for building strings in one thread. <code>StringBuffer</code> is mutable and synchronized, so it is thread safe but slower.',
      '<p>Concatenating with <code>+</code> inside a loop creates a new string on every pass, which is O(n squared) work overall. Use a <code>StringBuilder</code> instead. Outside loops, the compiler already optimises simple <code>+</code> expressions.</p>'),
    Q('reverse-string', 'Strings', 'e',
      'Write a program to reverse a string without using <code>StringBuilder.reverse()</code>.',
      'Swap characters from both ends of a char array, moving inwards.',
      'Convert the string to a char array, swap the first and last characters, move both pointers inwards until they meet, and build a new string from the array.',
      '<p>This runs in O(n) time and O(n) extra space for the array. Interviewers often follow up by asking you to reverse each word in a sentence, or to check whether the string is a palindrome by comparing it with its reverse.</p>',
      kind='Coding',
      acode='static String reverse(String s) {\n    char[] c = s.toCharArray();\n    for (int i = 0, j = c.length - 1; i < j; i++, j--) {\n        char t = c[i]; c[i] = c[j]; c[j] = t;\n    }\n    return new String(c);\n}',
      follow=['Reverse the order of words in a sentence', 'Check whether a string is a palindrome ignoring case']),
    Q('char-count', 'Strings', 'm',
      'Count how many times each character appears in <code>"automation"</code>, keeping the order of first appearance.',
      'Which map keeps insertion order?',
      'Loop over the characters and use a <code>LinkedHashMap&lt;Character, Integer&gt;</code> with <code>merge(c, 1, Integer::sum)</code>. The output is <code>{a=2, u=1, t=2, o=2, m=1, i=1, n=1}</code>.',
      '<p>A <code>HashMap</code> would also count correctly but the order would not be predictable. With streams you can write <code>s.chars().mapToObj(c -&gt; (char) c).collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Function.identity(), LinkedHashMap::new, Collectors.counting()))</code>.</p><p>A common follow up is to find the first character that does not repeat, which is the first key with a count of 1.</p>',
      kind='Coding',
      acode='Map<Character, Integer> count = new LinkedHashMap<>();\nfor (char c : "automation".toCharArray()) {\n    count.merge(c, 1, Integer::sum);\n}\nSystem.out.println(count); // {a=2, u=1, t=2, o=2, m=1, i=1, n=1}'),
    Q('anagram', 'Strings', 'm',
      'How do you check if two strings are anagrams?',
      'Either sort both, or count letters.',
      'Two strings are anagrams if they contain the same letters with the same counts. Sort the character arrays of both and compare them, which is O(n log n), or count each letter in an <code>int[26]</code> array, adding for the first string and subtracting for the second, which is O(n).',
      '<p>Clarify with the interviewer whether case and spaces matter. Normalise first with <code>toLowerCase()</code> and <code>replaceAll("\\\\s", "")</code>.</p>',
      kind='Coding',
      acode='static boolean isAnagram(String a, String b) {\n    if (a.length() != b.length()) return false;\n    int[] n = new int[26];\n    for (int i = 0; i < a.length(); i++) {\n        n[a.charAt(i) - \'a\']++;\n        n[b.charAt(i) - \'a\']--;\n    }\n    for (int x : n) if (x != 0) return false;\n    return true;\n}'),
    Q('string-switch-output', 'Strings', 'h', 'What does this print?',
      'Evaluation goes left to right, and once a String is involved, + means joining.',
      'It prints <code>30Java1020</code>. The first <code>10 + 20</code> is added as numbers giving 30. After that the left side is a String, so the rest of the <code>+</code> operators join text, adding <code>10</code> and then <code>20</code> as text.',
      '<p>Wrap the second pair in brackets, <code>("" + (10 + 20))</code>, if you want the sum instead of the joined digits.</p>',
      kind='Output',
      code='public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(10 + 20 + "Java" + 10 + 20);\n    }\n}',
      acode='30Java1020'),

    # ---------------- Exceptions ----------------
    Q('checked-unchecked', 'Exceptions', 'e',
      'What is the difference between checked and unchecked exceptions? Give examples.',
      'One kind the compiler forces you to handle.',
      'Checked exceptions extend <code>Exception</code> but not <code>RuntimeException</code>, and the compiler forces you to catch them or declare them with <code>throws</code>. Examples are <code>IOException</code> and <code>SQLException</code>. Unchecked exceptions extend <code>RuntimeException</code> and are not checked at compile time, for example <code>NullPointerException</code>, <code>ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException</code> and <code>IllegalArgumentException</code>.',
      '<p><code>Error</code> types such as <code>OutOfMemoryError</code> are also unchecked but signal problems the application should not try to recover from. In Selenium, <code>NoSuchElementException</code>, <code>TimeoutException</code> and <code>StaleElementReferenceException</code> are all unchecked because they extend <code>WebDriverException</code>, which is a <code>RuntimeException</code>.</p>',
      points=['Checked: compile time, recoverable, IOException', 'Unchecked: run time, programming errors, NPE', 'Error: serious JVM problems, do not catch']),
    Q('throw-throws', 'Exceptions', 'e',
      'What is the difference between <code>throw</code> and <code>throws</code>?',
      'One is a statement, the other is part of a method signature.',
      '<code>throw</code> is a statement that throws one exception object, as in <code>throw new IllegalStateException("cart empty")</code>. <code>throws</code> appears in a method declaration and lists the checked exceptions the method may pass to its caller.',
      '<p>You can throw only one exception at a time, but a method can declare several in its <code>throws</code> clause.</p>'),
    Q('finally-return', 'Exceptions', 'h', 'What does this method return?',
      'A return in finally replaces whatever the try block was going to return.',
      'It returns <code>3</code>. The <code>finally</code> block always runs after <code>try</code> and <code>catch</code>, and a <code>return</code> inside <code>finally</code> overrides the earlier return value. It would even swallow an exception thrown in <code>try</code>, which is why returning from <code>finally</code> is a bad practice.',
      '<p><code>finally</code> does not run only when the JVM stops, for example with <code>System.exit</code>, a crash or a killed process. Changing a primitive variable in <code>finally</code> after <code>return x</code> does not change the returned value, because the value was already saved.</p>',
      kind='Output',
      code='public class Main {\n    static int test() {\n        try { return 1; }\n        catch (Exception e) { return 2; }\n        finally { return 3; }\n    }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(test());\n    }\n}',
      acode='3'),
    Q('try-resources', 'Exceptions', 'm',
      'What is try with resources and what is a suppressed exception?',
      'The resource must implement a particular interface.',
      'Try with resources declares objects that implement <code>AutoCloseable</code> in brackets after <code>try</code>, and Java closes them automatically in reverse order, even when an exception is thrown. If closing also throws, that second exception is attached to the first as a suppressed exception, which you can read with <code>getSuppressed()</code>.',
      '<p>It replaces long <code>finally</code> blocks that close streams, JDBC connections and readers. In automation it is ideal for reading Excel or CSV test data files.</p>',
      acode='try (BufferedReader r = Files.newBufferedReader(Path.of("users.csv"))) {\n    r.lines().forEach(System.out::println);\n} // r is closed here'),
    Q('custom-exception', 'Exceptions', 'm',
      'How do you create a custom exception, and should it be checked or unchecked?',
      'Extend one of two classes depending on whether the caller can recover.',
      'Extend <code>Exception</code> for a checked exception or <code>RuntimeException</code> for an unchecked one, and add constructors that take a message and a cause. Choose checked when the caller can reasonably recover, and unchecked for programming errors or framework failures.',
      '<p>Always pass the original exception as the cause so the stack trace is not lost. In a framework you might throw <code>FrameworkException("Config key missing: baseUrl", e)</code> as an unchecked exception so that every test method does not need a <code>throws</code> clause.</p>',
      acode='public class FrameworkException extends RuntimeException {\n    public FrameworkException(String msg) { super(msg); }\n    public FrameworkException(String msg, Throwable cause) { super(msg, cause); }\n}'),
    Q('catch-order', 'Exceptions', 'm',
      'Does this compile? <code>try { ... } catch (Exception e) { } catch (IOException e) { }</code>',
      'Think about which catch block could ever be reached.',
      'No. The second catch is unreachable because <code>IOException</code> is a subclass of <code>Exception</code>, which the first block already catches. Catch blocks must go from the most specific type to the most general.',
      '<p>Since Java 7 you can also use a multi catch, <code>catch (IOException | SQLException e)</code>, as long as the types are not subclasses of each other.</p>'),

    # ---------------- Collections ----------------
    Q('collection-hierarchy', 'Collections', 'e',
      'Describe the main interfaces of the Collections framework.',
      'Three interfaces extend Collection, and one important one does not.',
      '<code>Collection</code> is the root, with <code>List</code> (ordered, allows duplicates), <code>Set</code> (no duplicates) and <code>Queue</code> (processing order) extending it. <code>Map</code> stores key value pairs and does not extend <code>Collection</code>.',
      '<table><tr><th>Interface</th><th>Common classes</th></tr><tr><td>List</td><td>ArrayList, LinkedList</td></tr><tr><td>Set</td><td>HashSet, LinkedHashSet, TreeSet</td></tr><tr><td>Queue and Deque</td><td>ArrayDeque, PriorityQueue, LinkedList</td></tr><tr><td>Map</td><td>HashMap, LinkedHashMap, TreeMap, ConcurrentHashMap</td></tr></table><p><code>Collection</code> is the interface. <code>Collections</code> is a utility class with static methods such as <code>sort</code> and <code>unmodifiableList</code>.</p>'),
    Q('arraylist-linkedlist', 'Collections', 'e',
      'When would you use <code>ArrayList</code> and when <code>LinkedList</code>?',
      'Think about index access compared with inserting in the middle.',
      '<code>ArrayList</code> uses a resizable array, so getting by index is O(1) and adding at the end is amortised O(1), but inserting or removing in the middle shifts elements, which is O(n). <code>LinkedList</code> is a doubly linked list, so adding or removing at the ends is O(1) but getting by index is O(n). In practice <code>ArrayList</code> is the default choice.',
      '<p><code>ArrayList</code> also wins on memory and cache friendliness because the elements sit next to each other. If you need a queue or stack, <code>ArrayDeque</code> is usually faster than <code>LinkedList</code>.</p>'),
    Q('hashmap-internal', 'Collections', 'h',
      'How does <code>HashMap</code> work internally?',
      'Buckets, hash codes, equals, load factor, and what happens when one bucket gets long.',
      'A <code>HashMap</code> keeps an array of buckets. <code>put</code> computes the key\'s <code>hashCode</code>, spreads its bits and picks a bucket index. If the bucket is empty the entry goes in. If not, <code>equals</code> is used to find a matching key to replace, otherwise the entry is added to the bucket\'s list. When the size passes capacity times the load factor (16 times 0.75 by default) the array doubles and entries are redistributed.',
      '<p>Since Java 8, a bucket with more than 8 entries turns into a red black tree when the table has at least 64 buckets, so the worst case lookup becomes O(log n) instead of O(n). <code>HashMap</code> allows one <code>null</code> key, which always goes to bucket 0, and many <code>null</code> values. It is not thread safe.</p><p>Mutable keys are dangerous. If a field used in <code>hashCode</code> changes after insertion, the entry sits in the wrong bucket and cannot be found again.</p>',
      points=['Average get and put: O(1)', 'Default capacity 16, load factor 0.75', 'Treeify threshold 8 since Java 8', 'One null key allowed'],
      follow=['What is a hash collision?', 'Why is the capacity always a power of two?']),
    Q('hashmap-hashtable-chm', 'Collections', 'm',
      'Compare <code>HashMap</code>, <code>Hashtable</code> and <code>ConcurrentHashMap</code>.',
      'Thread safety, null handling and how locking is done.',
      '<code>HashMap</code> is not synchronized and allows one null key and null values. <code>Hashtable</code> is a legacy class where every method is synchronized on the whole table, and it allows no nulls. <code>ConcurrentHashMap</code> is thread safe with fine grained locking and lock free reads, allows no nulls, and is the right choice for shared maps.',
      '<p>Iterators of <code>ConcurrentHashMap</code> are weakly consistent, so they never throw <code>ConcurrentModificationException</code>. Use its atomic methods such as <code>computeIfAbsent</code> and <code>merge</code> instead of a check then put sequence, which is a race condition.</p>'),
    Q('set-types', 'Collections', 'e',
      'What is the difference between <code>HashSet</code>, <code>LinkedHashSet</code> and <code>TreeSet</code>?',
      'All three remove duplicates, so compare how they order elements.',
      '<code>HashSet</code> has no guaranteed order and O(1) operations. <code>LinkedHashSet</code> keeps insertion order. <code>TreeSet</code> keeps elements sorted, either naturally or by a <code>Comparator</code>, with O(log n) operations.',
      '<p>A <code>HashSet</code> is backed by a <code>HashMap</code> whose values are a dummy object. <code>TreeSet</code> uses <code>compareTo</code> rather than <code>equals</code> to spot duplicates, so a comparator that returns 0 for two different items will drop one of them.</p>'),
    Q('fail-fast', 'Collections', 'm',
      'What is <code>ConcurrentModificationException</code> and how do you remove items while looping?',
      'Iterators keep a count of changes to the list.',
      'Fail fast iterators throw <code>ConcurrentModificationException</code> when the collection is changed structurally during iteration by anything other than the iterator itself. Remove safely with <code>iterator.remove()</code>, with <code>list.removeIf(...)</code>, or by looping over a copy.',
      '<p>The exception can appear even in a single thread, for example when you call <code>list.remove(x)</code> inside an enhanced for loop. Concurrent collections such as <code>CopyOnWriteArrayList</code> use fail safe iterators that work on a snapshot.</p>',
      acode='List<String> names = new ArrayList<>(List.of("ann", "", "bob", ""));\nnames.removeIf(String::isBlank);   // [ann, bob]'),
    Q('comparable-comparator', 'Collections', 'm',
      'What is the difference between <code>Comparable</code> and <code>Comparator</code>?',
      'One lives inside the class, the other outside it.',
      '<code>Comparable</code> is implemented by the class itself with <code>compareTo</code> and defines its single natural order. <code>Comparator</code> is a separate object with <code>compare</code>, so you can define many orders without changing the class.',
      '<p>Java 8 made comparators easy to build: <code>Comparator.comparing(Product::price).reversed().thenComparing(Product::name)</code>. In UI tests this is how you check that a product list is sorted by price: read the prices, sort a copy, and assert that the two lists are equal.</p>',
      kind='Scenario'),
    Q('duplicates-array', 'Collections', 'e',
      'Find the duplicate elements in an integer array.',
      'A Set refuses to add something it already contains.',
      'Loop through the array and try to add each value to a <code>HashSet</code>. When <code>add</code> returns false the value is a duplicate, so put it in a second set of duplicates. This is O(n) time and O(n) space.',
      '<p>Other options are sorting and comparing neighbours, which is O(n log n) with no extra set, or a stream with <code>groupingBy</code> and counting.</p>',
      kind='Coding',
      acode='int[] a = {4, 2, 7, 2, 4, 9};\nSet<Integer> seen = new HashSet<>(), dup = new LinkedHashSet<>();\nfor (int x : a) if (!seen.add(x)) dup.add(x);\nSystem.out.println(dup); // [2, 4]'),

    # ---------------- Java 8 and later ----------------
    Q('java8-features', 'Java 8 and later', 'e',
      'What are the main features introduced in Java 8?',
      'Most of them are about functional programming, plus a new date API.',
      'Lambda expressions, functional interfaces, the Stream API, method references, <code>default</code> and <code>static</code> methods in interfaces, <code>Optional</code>, and the new <code>java.time</code> date and time API.',
      '<p>Later versions worth mentioning: <code>var</code> for local variables (10), the HTTP client (11), switch expressions (14), text blocks (15), records and pattern matching for <code>instanceof</code> (16), sealed classes (17), and virtual threads, record patterns and sequenced collections (21).</p>'),
    Q('functional-interface', 'Java 8 and later', 'e',
      'What is a functional interface? Name some built in ones.',
      'Count the abstract methods.',
      'An interface with exactly one abstract method, so a lambda can implement it. Built in examples are <code>Runnable</code>, <code>Comparator</code>, <code>Predicate&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>Function&lt;T,R&gt;</code>, <code>Consumer&lt;T&gt;</code> and <code>Supplier&lt;T&gt;</code>.',
      '<p>It may still have any number of <code>default</code> and <code>static</code> methods. The <code>@FunctionalInterface</code> annotation is optional but makes the compiler reject a second abstract method. Selenium\'s <code>ExpectedCondition</code> used with <code>WebDriverWait.until</code> is a functional interface, so you can pass a lambda such as <code>d -&gt; d.findElement(By.id("ok")).isDisplayed()</code>.</p>'),
    Q('stream-map-flatmap', 'Java 8 and later', 'm',
      'What is the difference between <code>map</code> and <code>flatMap</code> in streams?',
      'One gives one result per item, the other can give many and flattens them.',
      '<code>map</code> turns each element into exactly one new element. <code>flatMap</code> turns each element into a stream and then joins all those streams into one flat stream.',
      '<p>For a list of orders where each order has a list of items, <code>orders.stream().map(Order::items)</code> gives a stream of lists, while <code>orders.stream().flatMap(o -&gt; o.items().stream())</code> gives a stream of items.</p>'),
    Q('stream-lazy', 'Java 8 and later', 'h',
      'What are intermediate and terminal operations, and what does lazy evaluation mean?',
      'Nothing happens until something asks for the result.',
      'Intermediate operations such as <code>filter</code>, <code>map</code> and <code>sorted</code> return a new stream and do no work on their own. Terminal operations such as <code>collect</code>, <code>forEach</code>, <code>count</code> and <code>findFirst</code> start the pipeline and produce a result. Lazy means elements are pulled through the chain only when the terminal operation needs them.',
      '<p>Laziness enables short circuiting. In <code>stream.filter(...).findFirst()</code> processing stops at the first match. A stream can be consumed only once, and a second terminal operation throws <code>IllegalStateException</code>.</p>'),
    Q('stream-coding', 'Java 8 and later', 'm',
      'Using streams, get the names of employees with salary above 50000, in upper case, sorted.',
      'filter, then map, then sorted, then collect.',
      'Filter by salary, map to the name in upper case, sort, and collect to a list.',
      '<p>Other stream tasks interviewers like: the second highest salary (<code>sorted(Comparator.reverseOrder()).skip(1).findFirst()</code>), grouping employees by department with <code>Collectors.groupingBy</code>, and joining names with <code>Collectors.joining(", ")</code>.</p>',
      kind='Coding',
      acode='List<String> names = employees.stream()\n    .filter(e -> e.salary() > 50000)\n    .map(e -> e.name().toUpperCase())\n    .sorted()\n    .toList();'),
    Q('optional', 'Java 8 and later', 'm',
      'What problem does <code>Optional</code> solve and how should it be used?',
      'It makes "there may be no value" part of the return type.',
      '<code>Optional</code> represents a value that may be missing, so callers are forced to deal with the empty case instead of getting a <code>NullPointerException</code>. Use it as a return type and read it with <code>orElse</code>, <code>orElseGet</code>, <code>orElseThrow</code>, <code>map</code> or <code>ifPresent</code>.',
      '<p>Avoid calling <code>get()</code> without checking, since that just moves the exception. Do not use <code>Optional</code> for fields or method parameters. Note that <code>orElse(x)</code> always evaluates <code>x</code>, while <code>orElseGet(() -&gt; x)</code> evaluates it only when the value is missing.</p>'),

    # ---------------- Multithreading ----------------
    Q('thread-create', 'Multithreading', 'e',
      'What are the ways to create a thread? Which is better?',
      'One way uses inheritance, one uses an interface, and modern code avoids both directly.',
      'Extend <code>Thread</code> and override <code>run</code>, or implement <code>Runnable</code> (or <code>Callable</code> to return a value) and pass it to a thread. Implementing <code>Runnable</code> is better because your class can still extend another class and the task is separate from the thread. In real code, submit tasks to an <code>ExecutorService</code>.',
      '<p>Calling <code>run()</code> directly runs the code in the current thread. Only <code>start()</code> creates a new thread. Starting the same thread twice throws <code>IllegalThreadStateException</code>. Java 21 adds virtual threads with <code>Thread.ofVirtual().start(task)</code> or <code>Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()</code>.</p>'),
    Q('synchronized-volatile', 'Multithreading', 'h',
      'What is the difference between <code>synchronized</code> and <code>volatile</code>?',
      'One gives visibility only, the other gives visibility and mutual exclusion.',
      '<code>volatile</code> guarantees that every read sees the latest write to that variable, but it does not make compound actions such as <code>count++</code> atomic. <code>synchronized</code> gives both visibility and mutual exclusion, so only one thread at a time runs the guarded block.',
      '<p>A <code>volatile boolean running</code> flag to stop a worker is a correct use of volatile. A counter shared by threads needs <code>synchronized</code>, a <code>Lock</code> or an <code>AtomicInteger</code>, because <code>count++</code> is a read, an add and a write that can interleave between threads.</p>'),
    Q('deadlock', 'Multithreading', 'h',
      'What is a deadlock and how do you prevent it?',
      'Two threads each hold one lock and wait for the other\'s lock.',
      'A deadlock happens when two or more threads each hold a lock and wait forever for a lock the other one holds. Prevent it by always acquiring locks in the same global order, holding locks for as short a time as possible, or using <code>tryLock</code> with a timeout.',
      '<p>You can find a deadlock with a thread dump from <code>jstack</code> or JConsole, which reports "Found one Java-level deadlock" and shows which threads hold which monitors.</p>'),
    Q('threadlocal-driver', 'Multithreading', 'h',
      'Your Selenium tests fail randomly when run in parallel. Browsers close in the middle of other tests. What is the likely cause and fix?',
      'Where is the WebDriver stored, and how many threads can see it?',
      'The driver is most likely stored in a <code>static</code> field shared by all threads, so one test overwrites or quits another test\'s browser. Store it in a <code>ThreadLocal&lt;WebDriver&gt;</code> so each thread has its own driver, and call <code>remove()</code> after quitting.',
      '<p>Also check for other shared mutable state, such as static page objects, shared test data and non thread safe report writers. Calling <code>remove()</code> matters because thread pools reuse threads, and a leftover value would leak into the next test.</p>',
      kind='Scenario',
      acode='public final class DriverManager {\n    private static final ThreadLocal<WebDriver> DRIVER = new ThreadLocal<>();\n    public static WebDriver get() { return DRIVER.get(); }\n    public static void set(WebDriver d) { DRIVER.set(d); }\n    public static void quit() {\n        if (DRIVER.get() != null) { DRIVER.get().quit(); DRIVER.remove(); }\n    }\n}'),
    Q('executor', 'Multithreading', 'm',
      'What is the Executor framework and why use it instead of creating threads?',
      'Think about reuse, limits and getting results back.',
      'The Executor framework separates submitting tasks from running them. An <code>ExecutorService</code> keeps a pool of reusable threads, limits how many run at once, and returns a <code>Future</code> or <code>CompletableFuture</code> so you can get results or exceptions.',
      '<p>Creating a raw thread per task is expensive and has no upper bound. Always call <code>shutdown()</code> on a pool you created, or use try with resources since <code>ExecutorService</code> is <code>AutoCloseable</code> from Java 19.</p>'),

    # ---------------- Generics and misc ----------------
    Q('generics-erasure', 'Generics and more', 'h',
      'What is type erasure in generics?',
      'Generic type information is checked by the compiler and then removed.',
      'Generics are checked at compile time and then erased, so at run time a <code>List&lt;String&gt;</code> and a <code>List&lt;Integer&gt;</code> are both just <code>List</code>. The compiler inserts casts where needed.',
      '<p>Because of erasure you cannot write <code>new T()</code>, cannot use <code>instanceof List&lt;String&gt;</code>, cannot create generic arrays such as <code>new T[10]</code>, and cannot overload two methods that differ only in their generic parameter.</p>'),
    Q('wildcards', 'Generics and more', 'x',
      'Explain <code>? extends T</code> and <code>? super T</code> with the PECS rule.',
      'Producer extends, consumer super.',
      'Use <code>? extends T</code> when the collection produces values you read as <code>T</code>, and <code>? super T</code> when it consumes values of type <code>T</code> that you add. You cannot safely add to a <code>List&lt;? extends Number&gt;</code>, and you can only read <code>Object</code> from a <code>List&lt;? super Integer&gt;</code>.',
      '<p><code>Collections.copy(List&lt;? super T&gt; dest, List&lt;? extends T&gt; src)</code> is the textbook example: it reads from the producer and writes to the consumer.</p>'),
    Q('serialization', 'Generics and more', 'm',
      'What is serialization? What do <code>transient</code> and <code>serialVersionUID</code> do?',
      'Turning an object into bytes, and then back.',
      'Serialization converts an object into a byte stream so it can be saved or sent, and deserialization rebuilds it. The class must implement <code>Serializable</code>. <code>transient</code> fields are skipped, which suits passwords or caches. <code>serialVersionUID</code> is a version number checked during deserialization, and a mismatch throws <code>InvalidClassException</code>.',
      '<p>Static fields are not serialized because they belong to the class. Java native serialization has security risks, so most modern systems use JSON with Jackson or Gson instead. In API automation you serialize POJOs into JSON request bodies and deserialize responses back into POJOs.</p>'),
    Q('singleton', 'Generics and more', 'h',
      'How do you write a thread safe Singleton?',
      'There are three popular answers: an enum, a holder class, and double checked locking with volatile.',
      'The simplest thread safe and serialization safe way is an <code>enum</code> with one constant. A lazy alternative is the holder idiom, where a static nested class holds the instance and the JVM\'s class loading guarantees it is created once.',
      '<p>Double checked locking also works, but the field must be <code>volatile</code>, otherwise another thread can see a partly built object. In frameworks a singleton is common for a config reader. For WebDriver, use ThreadLocal instead so parallel runs work.</p>',
      acode='public class Config {\n    private Config() { }\n    private static class Holder { static final Config INSTANCE = new Config(); }\n    public static Config get() { return Holder.INSTANCE; }\n}'),
    Q('records', 'Generics and more', 'm',
      'What is a <code>record</code> and when would you use it?',
      'A short way to write a data carrier class.',
      'A <code>record</code> (Java 16) is a final class for immutable data. Writing <code>record User(String name, int age) { }</code> gives you private final fields, a constructor, accessors <code>name()</code> and <code>age()</code>, and <code>equals</code>, <code>hashCode</code> and <code>toString</code>.',
      '<p>Records suit API response models, test data and value objects. You can add a compact constructor to validate input. A record cannot extend another class, but it can implement interfaces.</p>'),
    Q('framework-design', 'Generics and more', 'x',
      'Walk through how you would design a Java test automation framework.',
      'Layers: config, driver management, pages, tests, data, reporting, and CI.',
      'Split it into layers: a config reader for environments, a thread safe driver factory, Page Objects or components for UI, API clients for services, test data builders or providers, the tests themselves with TestNG or JUnit, utilities for waits and files, and reporting with logs and screenshots, all run from Maven or Gradle in CI.',
      '<ul><li>Use OOP deliberately: an abstract base page with shared waits, composition for reusable components, interfaces for drivers and reporters.</li><li>Keep tests free of locators and raw driver calls.</li><li>Use ThreadLocal for drivers so parallel runs are safe.</li><li>Read environment specific values from properties files or environment variables, never hard coded.</li><li>Fail with clear messages, and attach a screenshot and logs in a listener.</li></ul><p>Interviewers want to hear the reasons behind each choice, and what you would change as the suite grows to thousands of tests.</p>',
      kind='Design', follow=['How do you handle flaky tests?', 'How do you run the suite against several environments?']),
]
